  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four hydropower layouts and the main river impacts associated with each. All four diagrams show a river crossing with a power station and transmission lines, but they differ in how much water is stored and how flow is routed around the dam.\n\nThe ultralow-head run-of-river scheme appears to pass water directly through a low barrier with little visible storage, while still interrupting movement of fish or other species and trapping some sediment. The dam-integral scheme creates a clear upstream reservoir, with water released over the dam and impacts linked to impoundment, hydropeaking, and downstream temperature changes in addition to sediment retention and blocked movement.\n\nThe two diversion designs reroute water away from the natural channel to a powerhouse farther downstream. In the diversion-weir scheme, water is diverted from a low weir, leaving the bypassed reach with reduced discharge. The diversion-pondage scheme adds a larger storage pond behind the diversion dam, combining impoundment with reduced flow in the natural channel and downstream hydropeaking and thermal alteration.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights a trade-off: more storage and diversion generally correspond to a broader set of ecological and hydrological alterations.",
